    "q-47887": {
      "dom_id": "step-q47887",
      "name": "Question 2",
      "problem": "Given that $f(x)={x}^{2}$, the instantaneous rate of change ${f}^{′}(-1)$ can be computed using the limit of a difference quotient. The first few steps of this calculation are shown below:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(-1) & ={lim}_{h\\to 0}\\frac{f(-1+h)-f(-1)}{h} \\\\  & ={lim}_{h\\to 0}\\frac{(-1+h)^{2}-(-1)^{2}}{h} \\\\  & =…\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinish the calculation, and hence find ${f}^{′}(-1).$",
      "worked_solution": "By proceeding with the remaining calculations we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(-1) & ={lim}_{h\\to 0}\\frac{f(-1+h)-f(-1)}{h} \\\\  & ={lim}_{h\\to 0}\\frac{(-1+h)^{2}-(-1)^{2}}{h} \\\\  & ={lim}_{h\\to 0}\\frac{1-2h+{h}^{2}-1}{h} \\\\  & ={lim}_{h\\to 0}\\frac{h(-2+h)}{h} \\\\  & ={lim}_{h\\to 0}(-2+h) \\\\  & =-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, ${f}^{′}(-1)=-2.$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
